WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.916 --> 00:00:05.026
This mixing vessel works in batches: it makes one lot at a time,

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.454
and then starts again.

3
00:00:07.197 --> 00:00:11.539
One cycle is fill, heat, react or mix, cool, empty and clean.

4
00:00:12.538 --> 00:00:16.892
The batch cycle time is everything between two starts of the same vessel.

5
00:00:17.565 --> 00:00:21.165
So the waiting counts too: for an operator, for a lab result,

6
00:00:21.327 --> 00:00:23.115
or for a free tank downstream.

7
00:00:24.096 --> 00:00:28.427
The capacity of a vessel is the batch size divided by the cycle time.

8
00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:33.351
A 5-hour cycle of 4 tonnes gives 0.8 tonnes per hour.

9
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:38.533
If the vessel runs day and night, that is about 19 tonnes per day.

10
00:00:39.847 --> 00:00:45.025
An example: a paint mixing vessel that makes 3,000 kilograms per batch.

11
00:00:45.756 --> 00:00:47.451
Filling takes 40 minutes.

12
00:00:48.226 --> 00:00:53.125
Then come 90 minutes of dispersing: breaking up the pigment powder into the liquid.

13
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:58.272
After that, 50 minutes of waiting for the result of the QC sample,

14
00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:02.985
20 minutes to adjust, 60 minutes to fill off and 40 minutes to clean.

15
00:01:03.694 --> 00:01:06.434
Together 300 minutes: five hours.

16
00:01:07.426 --> 00:01:14.195
So the capacity is 3,000 kilograms divided by 5 hours: 600 kilograms per hour.

17
00:01:15.253 --> 00:01:17.587
Every minute in the cycle counts the same.

18
00:01:18.268 --> 00:01:21.379
Engineers like to shorten the reaction or the mixing.

19
00:01:22.078 --> 00:01:26.397
But filling, emptying, cleaning and waiting often take a third of the cycle,

20
00:01:26.548 --> 00:01:28.649
and they are usually easier to cut.

21
00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:31.255
Here it is even more than half.

22
00:01:32.541 --> 00:01:36.582
After dispersing, a sample goes to the lab, for quality control.

23
00:01:37.277 --> 00:01:41.678
Only after QC release, the lab’s approval, may the batch move on.

24
00:01:42.636 --> 00:01:46.955
But the lab serves the whole site, so the sample joins the back of the queue.

25
00:01:47.631 --> 00:01:50.139
All that time the vessel sits full and still.

26
00:01:50.886 --> 00:01:53.661
As engineers put it: the vessel is not slow,

27
00:01:53.788 --> 00:01:57.120
it spends 50 minutes of every batch waiting for the lab.

28
00:01:58.486 --> 00:02:01.458
Then cleaning, with CIP: cleaning in place.

29
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:06.650
A spray head in the vessel rinses the walls with hot water and detergent,

30
00:02:06.836 --> 00:02:09.379
and the liquid also runs through the pipes.

31
00:02:10.135 --> 00:02:12.295
Nobody has to take anything apart.

32
00:02:13.376 --> 00:02:16.174
Waiting and cleaning are often the two big ones.

33
00:02:16.874 --> 00:02:20.264
So faster lab tests, a measurement in the pipe itself,

34
00:02:20.415 --> 00:02:24.409
or a shorter cleaning programme add capacity, without a new vessel.

35
00:02:25.437 --> 00:02:27.329
Experts argue about cleaning.

36
00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:29.786
Do you clean after every batch,

37
00:02:29.972 --> 00:02:34.175
or run a campaign first: several batches of the same product in a row?

38
00:02:34.886 --> 00:02:39.031
A campaign saves cleaning time, but you must prove it stays hygienic,

39
00:02:39.217 --> 00:02:40.807
and you need longer storage.

40
00:02:42.123 --> 00:02:43.481
Back to the paint vessel.

41
00:02:44.218 --> 00:02:49.129
An inline viscosity meter is added: it measures in the pipe how thick the paint is.

42
00:02:49.858 --> 00:02:53.295
And the lab gets a fixed fast-track slot for the samples.

43
00:02:54.279 --> 00:02:58.250
The wait for quality control drops from 50 to 15 minutes.

44
00:02:58.971 --> 00:03:02.361
The new cycle takes 265 minutes.

45
00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:06.192
That is four hours and twenty-five minutes.

46
00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:12.397
3,000 kilograms in that time is 679 kilograms per hour:

47
00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.893
13 percent more from the same vessel,

48
00:03:15.009 --> 00:03:17.203
without anyone touching the dispersing.

49
00:03:18.504 --> 00:03:21.824
With several vessels in a row, the whole plant counts.

50
00:03:22.562 --> 00:03:26.753
A reactor of 6 hours per batch feeds a filter of 3 hours.

51
00:03:27.467 --> 00:03:32.076
The filter waits half the time: the plant makes one batch every 6 hours.

52
00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.918
Add a second reactor beside it, taking turns with the first.

53
00:03:37.732 --> 00:03:42.492
Then a batch comes out every 3 hours, as long as the filter keeps pace.

54
00:03:43.506 --> 00:03:46.327
The bottleneck is the stage that limits the whole plant.

55
00:03:47.085 --> 00:03:51.311
Divide the time of each stage by its number of parallel vessels:

56
00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:53.563
the longest result is the bottleneck.

57
00:03:54.259 --> 00:03:58.426
Here both stages are now at 3 hours, so there is no margin.

58
00:03:59.827 --> 00:04:01.627
How do you see where the time goes?

59
00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:06.193
Batch control often follows the standard ISA-88.

60
00:04:06.962 --> 00:04:10.271
In it, a recipe is split into ever smaller steps.

61
00:04:11.263 --> 00:04:16.441
The smallest step is called a phase: for example charge, heat, hold or transfer.

62
00:04:17.179 --> 00:04:21.648
Each phase has set values, such as an amount, a temperature or a time.

63
00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:27.654
The control system runs the phases, and logs each phase in a batch report.

64
00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:30.767
So ask for the batch reports,

65
00:04:30.929 --> 00:04:34.447
with the actual time per phase over the last 50 batches.

66
00:04:35.149 --> 00:04:38.144
The gap between the fastest and the slowest batches

67
00:04:38.237 --> 00:04:41.302
is often the biggest capacity gain on the whole site.

68
00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:46.608
A typical trap: vessels sitting full at shift change or in breaks,

69
00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.069
because nobody starts the next phase.

70
00:04:50.046 --> 00:04:53.239
So a sharp question in any batch plant is: per batch,

71
00:04:53.436 --> 00:04:58.069
how many minutes does this vessel sit waiting for a person, the lab or a free tank?
